verified.corrected_claim:
The core defect is real, and it is worse than the claim says. In _build_minnesota_dummies (puremacro/var/bvar.py:241-254) every (lag k, variable j) pair gets one dummy row. The inner `for eq` loop writes the same cell Xd[row, 1+(k-1)n+j] once per equation, so the eq=n-1 iteration decides its value. For every variable j<n-1 the cell ends up as cross_coeff = sigma_j k^l3/(l1 l2). Only Yd[row, j] is set to (k==1)*xd_coeff. As a result:
(a) the prior MEAN on the own first lag of variables 0..n-2 is l2 (0.5 by default), not 1;
(b) those variables get the tighter cross std in every equation, including their own;
(c) the last variable keeps mean 1 and own std, and the looser own std is also applied to its cross coefficients;
(d) the posterior depends on variable ordering.
The code paths affected are minnesota_gibbs (default lambda2=0.5), _minnesota_log_marginal_likelihood and minnesota_optimal_lambda (lambda2 grid (0.5, 1.0)). minnesota_posterior (bvar.py:139-173) is an independent per-equation loop and is correct. The lambda2 sub-claim is confirmed: under a Kronecker NIW prior the own/cross ratio cannot be represented, BGR require theta=1, and the code gives no warning.
The "circular" sub-claim is inaccurate. The validation case's compute calls minnesota_posterior, not _build_minnesota_dummies (the import at cases_var.py:113 is unused), so it is a cross-check between two internal implementations. Its real weakness is that it is pinned at lambda2=1.0 (cases_var.py:119,131). At that value cross_coeff == xd_coeff and the overwrite does nothing, so the case cannot detect the bug; at lambda2=0.5 the two paths differ by 0.037.

